pocket-motion-only: upstream ships this module to browsers, where its size is a download and opt-level "s" is the right trade; here Bun reads it from disk and rasterises every frame of a film with it, so throughput is the cost that counts.

0022 — the wasm core is compiled for speed: opt-level 3 and simd128.

- `engine/wasm/Cargo.toml`: `[profile.release] opt-level = 3`, was "s".
  LTO stays on.
- `engine/wasm/.cargo/config.toml` (new): `-C target-feature=+simd128` for
  `wasm32-unknown-unknown`, so LLVM may use 128-bit wasm SIMD where it
  vectorises the raster loops. Cargo reads the file because every build
  of this crate runs cargo in `engine/wasm` (`scripts/vendor.ts`, and
  upstream's `tools/wasm.ts`). A `RUSTFLAGS` in the environment replaces
  it (cargo does not merge the two) and builds the same pixels without
  SIMD.

Neither changes a pixel. Wasm SIMD's integer lanes are exact, its f32
lanes are IEEE single precision like the scalar ops, and LLVM reorders no
float arithmetic without fast-math flags, which Rust never sets; the
ledger's digests are the check, on macOS/arm64 and on Linux/x86-64 in CI.
Bun's JavaScriptCore compiles wasm SIMD on both.

The module grows from 312 KB to 513 KB with Rust 1.97.1 (give or take
the few hundred bytes a checkout's path adds to the panic locations it
carries): opt-level 3 alone makes it 496 KB, simd128 the rest.

`git apply` skips everything above the first `diff --git`.
diff --git a/engine/wasm/.cargo/config.toml b/engine/wasm/.cargo/config.toml
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..39095002
--- /dev/null
+++ b/engine/wasm/.cargo/config.toml
@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@
+# pocket-motion: 128-bit wasm SIMD for the raster loops LLVM vectorises.
+# Read because every build of this crate runs cargo in this directory; a
+# RUSTFLAGS in the environment replaces it rather than adding to it.
+[target.wasm32-unknown-unknown]
+rustflags = ["-C", "target-feature=+simd128"]
